1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,683
This is STAT 331, video 9. The topic is the estimate of the mean response in simple linear regression.

2
00:00:09,133 --> 00:00:24,429
After this video, you can estimate the mean response at any x value. You can derive its mean and its variance. You can build a confidence interval for it. And you can solve Tutorial 2, question 1, parts a to d.

3
00:00:24,879 --> 00:00:44,249
First, a short recap. The model says y i equals beta zero plus beta one x i plus an error. The errors are I I D normal with mean 0 and variance sigma squared. The four assumptions are linearity, independence, normality, and equal variance.

4
00:00:44,699 --> 00:01:00,550
The least squares estimates are beta one hat equals S x y over S x x, and beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat times x bar. For the brainhead data, the green line is the fitted line.

5
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:16,616
When we put sigma hat in place of sigma, beta one hat minus beta one, over its standard error, has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. This gives the confidence interval and the test for beta one.

6
00:01:17,066 --> 00:01:37,354
For the brainhead data, the standard error of beta one hat is 0.01307. The 95 percent confidence interval for beta one is from 0.2351 to 0.2866. Now we ask a new question about the line itself.

7
00:01:37,804 --> 00:01:44,183
The gray dashed line is the true line, beta zero plus beta one x. We do not know it.

8
00:01:44,633 --> 00:01:54,339
At each x, the responses are normal around the true line. The orange curves show this spread. The mean of y at each x is on the true line.

9
00:01:54,789 --> 00:02:11,323
Pick any value x zero. The mean outcome there is mu zero. Mu zero is the expected value of y zero, given x zero. It equals beta zero plus beta one x zero. It is a parameter, so it is unknown.

10
00:02:11,773 --> 00:02:29,202
We estimate it with the fitted line. Mu zero hat equals beta zero hat plus beta one hat x zero. It is the height of the fitted line at x zero. Because the fitted line is not the true line, mu zero hat is not exactly mu zero.

11
00:02:29,652 --> 00:02:35,391
Start from mu zero hat equals beta zero hat plus beta one hat x zero.

12
00:02:35,841 --> 00:02:56,833
Put in the formula for beta zero hat. Beta zero hat equals y bar minus beta one hat x bar. In the picture, the purple point is x bar, y bar. For the brainhead data, it is 3637.86 and 1284.26.

13
00:02:57,283 --> 00:03:17,656
Collect the two terms with beta one hat. Mu zero hat equals y bar, plus beta one hat times x zero minus x bar. In the picture, take x zero equal to 4500. The step from x bar to x zero is 862 cubic centimeters.

14
00:03:18,106 --> 00:03:30,458
The rise is beta one hat times 862. That is 0.2608 times 862, which is 224.9 grams.

15
00:03:30,908 --> 00:03:53,244
So mu zero hat is y bar, 1284.26, plus 224.9. That is 1509.1 grams. At x zero equal to x bar, the second term is zero, so mu zero hat equals y bar. This form is the key to the variance.

16
00:03:53,694 --> 00:03:59,774
Here is an illustration with our own simulation. The gray dashed line is one true line.

17
00:04:00,224 --> 00:04:09,888
We draw a sample of 10 points from this model and fit a line. Then we do it again with a new sample. Each sample gives a different green line.

18
00:04:10,338 --> 00:04:29,303
Here are 15 fitted lines. They cross near the middle, at x bar. The formula explains why. Mu zero hat is y bar plus beta one hat times x zero minus x bar. An error in the slope is multiplied by the distance x zero minus x bar.

19
00:04:29,753 --> 00:04:42,020
So the lines are close together near x bar, and far apart at the edges. The estimate of the mean is most precise at x bar. The variance formula will show this exactly.

20
00:04:42,470 --> 00:04:57,105
Next, the mean of mu zero hat. Mu zero hat is random, because it comes from the random sample. Its distribution is the green curve. Start with E of beta zero hat plus beta one hat x zero.

21
00:04:57,555 --> 00:05:07,560
Expectation is linear, and x zero is a constant. So this is E of beta zero hat, plus E of beta one hat times x zero.

22
00:05:08,010 --> 00:05:23,925
Video 6 showed that beta zero hat and beta one hat are unbiased. So the result is beta zero plus beta one x zero. That is mu zero. So mu zero hat is an unbiased estimator of mu zero.

23
00:05:24,375 --> 00:05:34,679
In repeated samples, each sample gives one value of mu zero hat. The blue marks are such values. They are centered at the true mean mu zero.

24
00:05:35,129 --> 00:05:44,153
Now the variance. Start from the second form: the variance of y bar plus beta one hat times x zero minus x bar.

25
00:05:44,603 --> 00:06:04,165
Write both parts as sums over the y i. Y bar is the sum of 1 over n times y i. Beta one hat is S x y over S x x, and that is the sum of x i minus x bar, over S x x, times y i. Video 6 showed this form.

26
00:06:04,615 --> 00:06:24,861
Put the two sums together. Mu zero hat is a weighted sum of the y i, with weight c i. C i equals 1 over n, plus x i minus x bar times x zero minus x bar, over S x x. The weights depend only on the x values, so they are constants.

27
00:06:25,311 --> 00:06:42,356
Look at the weights for a small data set: x equals 1 to 5. Then x bar is 3 and S x x is 10. At x zero equal to x bar, the second part is zero. Every weight is one fifth. Mu zero hat is just y bar.

28
00:06:42,806 --> 00:07:03,777
At x zero equal to 5, the weights are minus 0.2, 0, 0.2, 0.4, and 0.6. The point at the far end gets a large weight. The sum of the squared weights is 0.6, not 0.2. Large squared weights give a large variance.

29
00:07:04,227 --> 00:07:16,045
The y i are independent, and each has variance sigma squared. So the variance of the sum of c i y i is the sum of c i squared, times sigma squared.

30
00:07:16,495 --> 00:07:25,967
Square each weight. A square of a sum of two parts gives three terms: the first part squared, the second part squared, and two times the product.

31
00:07:26,417 --> 00:07:46,599
Now sum each term over i. The first term gives n times 1 over n squared, which is 1 over n. The second term contains the sum of x i minus x bar squared. That sum is S x x. So it becomes x zero minus x bar squared, over S x x.

32
00:07:47,049 --> 00:07:55,411
The cross term contains the sum of x i minus x bar. This sum is always zero. So the cross term is zero.

33
00:07:55,861 --> 00:08:18,261
The result: the variance of mu zero hat is sigma squared times the quantity 1 over n, plus x zero minus x bar squared over S x x. Check it on the small data set at x zero equal to 5. 1 over 5 plus 4 over 10 is 0.6. That equals the sum of the squared weights.

34
00:08:18,711 --> 00:08:35,607
The slide asks why mu zero hat is normal. Mu zero hat is a linear combination of the y i, with constant weights. The y i are independent and normal. A linear combination of independent normal variables is normal.

35
00:08:36,057 --> 00:08:41,881
So mu zero hat is normal, with mean mu zero and the variance from the last slide.

36
00:08:42,331 --> 00:08:53,958
Subtract the mean and divide by the standard deviation. The result has a standard normal distribution. But sigma is unknown, so we cannot use this directly.

37
00:08:54,408 --> 00:09:09,000
Put sigma hat in place of sigma, by the same logic as for beta one hat. The result has a t distribution with n minus 2 degrees of freedom. The denominator is the standard error of mu zero hat.

38
00:09:09,450 --> 00:09:19,221
Now build the interval. The t quantile t 1 minus alpha over 2, n minus 2, cuts off area alpha over 2 in each tail.

39
00:09:19,671 --> 00:09:28,609
So the probability that the standardized statistic is between minus t and t is 1 minus alpha. This is the green area.

40
00:09:29,059 --> 00:09:43,118
Multiply the inequality by the standard error, and solve for mu zero. Mu zero is between mu zero hat minus t times the standard error, and mu zero hat plus t times the standard error.

41
00:09:43,568 --> 00:10:07,589
So a 100 times 1 minus alpha percent confidence interval for mu zero is mu zero hat plus or minus t times sigma hat times the square root of 1 over n plus x zero minus x bar squared over S x x. It has the same shape as the interval for beta one: estimate, plus or minus t, times standard error.

42
00:10:08,039 --> 00:10:15,335
The green curve is the standard error of mu zero hat for the brainhead data, as a function of x zero.

43
00:10:15,785 --> 00:10:33,300
At x zero equal to x bar, the second term is zero. The standard error is sigma hat over root n, which is 72.35 over root 236. That is 4.71 grams. This is the smallest value.

44
00:10:33,750 --> 00:10:54,059
Away from x bar, the term x zero minus x bar squared over S x x grows. At 3000 the standard error is 9.57. At 4500 it is 12.21. This is the fan of lines from the simulation, now as a formula.

45
00:10:54,509 --> 00:11:06,819
The formula also shows how to get a precise estimate. A larger sample size n makes 1 over n small. A larger spread of the x values makes S x x large.

46
00:11:07,269 --> 00:11:30,138
Here is an example with the brainhead data, at x zero equal to 4000 cubic centimeters. First, the point estimate. 335.4323 plus 0.2608207 times 4000 is 1378.72 grams.

47
00:11:30,588 --> 00:11:51,196
Second, the two parts under the square root. 1 over 236 is 0.00424. x zero minus x bar is 362.14. Its square, over S x x of about 30.6 million, is 0.00428.

48
00:11:51,646 --> 00:12:06,366
Third, the standard error. 72.35 times the square root of the sum is 6.68 grams. The t quantile with 234 degrees of freedom is 1.970.

49
00:12:06,816 --> 00:12:25,675
Fourth, the interval. 1378.72 plus or minus 1.970 times 6.68 gives 1365.56 to 1391.87 grams.

50
00:12:26,125 --> 00:12:42,893
Interpret it for the mean. We are 95 percent confident that the mean brain weight of all people with head size 4000 is between 1365.6 and 1391.9 grams.

51
00:12:43,343 --> 00:12:53,220
The slides compute the interval at many values of x zero, from the smallest head size to the largest. Here are the data and the fitted line.

52
00:12:53,670 --> 00:13:01,286
Join the lower limits and the upper limits. This gives a 95 percent confidence band for the mean response.

53
00:13:01,736 --> 00:13:20,147
Why does the band get wider toward the edges? At x bar, the half width is 9.3 grams. At the smallest x, it is 24 grams, and at the largest x, it is 30 grams. The term x zero minus x bar squared grows at the edges.

54
00:13:20,597 --> 00:13:39,967
Why do most points fall outside the lines? The band shows where the mean is. It does not show where single people are. A single y has its own error, with standard deviation about 72 grams. Here only 41 of the 236 points are inside the band.

55
00:13:40,417 --> 00:13:52,428
Even if we knew the mean exactly, new points would not fall on the line. To predict a new response, we must add the error of the new point. That is the topic of the next video.

56
00:13:52,878 --> 00:14:15,875
Now Tutorial 2, question 1. A hospital measures satisfaction, y, and illness severity, x, for 25 patients. The question gives x bar, 50.48, and S x x, 532.24, and the regression output. We answer parts a to d. Part e and f are about prediction, in video 10.

57
00:14:16,325 --> 00:14:35,205
This is the coefficient table from the question. The Estimate column gives beta zero hat, 179.1312, and beta one hat, minus 2.3283. The next columns are the standard error, the t value, and the p-value.

58
00:14:35,655 --> 00:14:57,202
The residual standard error is sigma hat, 13.52, on 23 degrees of freedom. So n is 25. As a check, 13.52 over the square root of 532.24 is 0.586. This matches the standard error of the slope.

59
00:14:57,652 --> 00:15:12,180
Pause the video and try it. Part a asks for the fitted line. Read the two estimates from the table. Y hat equals 179.1312 minus 2.3283 x.

60
00:15:12,630 --> 00:15:31,382
The line passes through x bar, y bar. So we can also find y bar: 179.1312 minus 2.3283 times 50.48 is 61.60. We use this point again in part d.

61
00:15:31,832 --> 00:15:48,387
Pause the video and try it. Part b asks if satisfaction depends on severity. Write it as a test. The null hypothesis is beta one equals zero: no linear relation. The alternative is beta one is not zero.

62
00:15:48,837 --> 00:16:04,175
Under the null hypothesis, beta one hat over its standard error has a t distribution with 23 degrees of freedom. At the 5 percent level, the critical values are plus and minus 2.069.

63
00:16:04,625 --> 00:16:16,401
The observed value is minus 2.3283 over 0.5859, which is minus 3.974. It is far in the left tail.

64
00:16:16,851 --> 00:16:34,537
The p-value is two times the probability that T is larger than 3.974. The table gives 0.000601. This is less than 0.05, so we reject the null hypothesis.

65
00:16:34,987 --> 00:16:41,536
Conclusion: there is strong evidence that the mean satisfaction depends on the severity of the illness.

66
00:16:41,986 --> 00:16:59,522
Pause the video and try it. Part c asks for an interpretation and a confidence interval. Compare two groups of patients whose severity differs by 1. The estimated mean satisfaction of the group with higher severity is 2.33 units lower.

67
00:16:59,972 --> 00:17:07,823
For a 95 percent interval with 23 degrees of freedom, the t quantile is 2.0687.

68
00:17:08,273 --> 00:17:21,457
The interval is minus 2.3283 plus or minus 2.0687 times 0.5859. The half width is 1.212.

69
00:17:21,907 --> 00:17:31,400
So the 95 percent confidence interval for beta one is from minus 3.540 to minus 1.116.

70
00:17:31,850 --> 00:17:43,263
The whole interval is below zero. Zero is not in it. This agrees with the test in part b, which rejected beta one equals zero at the 5 percent level.

71
00:17:43,713 --> 00:17:56,257
Pause the video and try it. Part d asks for the estimated mean satisfaction at severity 55, with a 95 percent confidence interval. Here is the fitted line.

72
00:17:56,707 --> 00:18:08,185
First, the estimate. 179.1312 minus 2.3283 times 55 is 51.07.

73
00:18:08,635 --> 00:18:37,989
Second, the standard error. x zero minus x bar is 55 minus 50.48, which is 4.52. Its square is 20.43. Divide by 532.24 to get 0.0384. Add 1 over 25, which is 0.04. The square root of the sum is 0.280. Times 13.52, the standard error is 3.785.

74
00:18:38,439 --> 00:18:57,831
Third, the interval. The t quantile is 2.0687 again. 2.0687 times 3.785 is 7.83. So the interval is from 43.24 to 58.91. The blue bar shows it.

75
00:18:58,281 --> 00:19:17,503
Interpret it for the mean of a group, not for one patient. We are 95 percent confident that the mean satisfaction of all patients with severity 55 is between 43.24 and 58.91. The green band shows the interval at each severity.

76
00:19:17,953 --> 00:19:32,865
To recap. The mean response at x zero is beta zero plus beta one x zero. We estimate it with the fitted line, which also equals y bar plus beta one hat times x zero minus x bar.

77
00:19:33,315 --> 00:19:49,933
The estimate is unbiased. Its variance is sigma squared times 1 over n plus x zero minus x bar squared over S x x. It is normal, because it is a linear combination of independent normal y values.

78
00:19:50,383 --> 00:19:59,365
The confidence interval is mu zero hat plus or minus t with n minus 2 degrees of freedom, times the standard error.

79
00:19:59,815 --> 00:20:10,055
The interval is narrowest at x bar and wider at the edges. It is for the mean, not for single points. The next video predicts a new response.
